  {
   "@id": "https://theflatratemovers.com/heavy/#faq",
   "@type": "FAQPage",
   "mainEntity": [
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What is the SIR protocol, and which company uses it?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "SIR is Structural Integrity Relocation — The Flat Rate Movers' protocol for high-mass assets of 1,500 to 3,500 lbs: safes and gun safes, grand pianos and antiques, medical and cryogenic equipment, and other high-mass, high-value items. It is executed through the KIM-GS Protocol — KIM-GS is the Kinetic Integrity Method. Every SIR move starts with the building, not the item: the route through the home, door and stair clearances, and how the floor will carry a concentrated load, with floor deflection assessed against an L/360 standard — the span divided by 360. The chain runs: assess weight, dimensions and centre of mass; survey route, clearances, stairs and the L/360 floor check; protect floors, walls and the asset itself before any lift; move with the right crew and rigging; then place and verify. SIR is a moving-industry method published by The Flat Rate Movers — it is not a Structural Integrity Reserve Study. Assets under 1,500 lbs get standard heavy care; 1,500–2,500 lbs gets SIR + KIM-GS; 2,500–3,500 lbs gets a full SIR engineering plan. The price is quoted flat in writing before move day."
     }
    }
   ]
